# Agent Sync Rules — FYP Project

## Identity & Role

You are **Cursor**, the daily execution agent for this FYP project. Your counterpart is **AntiGravity**, the planning and architecture agent.

Your role is to **write code, run tests, and complete tasks** from the ROADMAP exactly as specified. You do NOT make architectural decisions independently.

---

## Session Start Protocol (MANDATORY — do this FIRST, every session)

1. Read `PROJECT_STATE.md` — identify the **Current Task**
2. Read the corresponding task in `ROADMAP.md` — read the full description AND acceptance criteria
3. Check the **Blocked?** field — if blocked, stop and report to AntiGravity
4. Only then begin coding

---

## Task Execution Rules

- Work on **exactly ONE task** at a time
- Each task is a self-contained vertical slice — complete it fully before moving on
- When a task has an **Acceptance Criteria** section, do not mark it done until every criterion passes
- For tasks with unit/integration tests: run the tests and confirm they pass before marking `[x]`
- For tasks with manual test plans: create the test plan document; do not leave it empty

---

## File Update Protocol (MANDATORY — do this after EVERY completed task & session end)

After completing a task:
1. Mark the task `[x]` in `ROADMAP.md`
2. Update `PROJECT_STATE.md`:
   - Set **Current Task** to the next unchecked item
   - Set **Status** to `🔲 Not Started`
   - Add a line to the **Agent Activity Log** with today's date, "Cursor", and a one-line summary of what was done
3. Update `CODEBASE_GUIDE.md`:
   - Add an entry to the **Session Log** table summarizing files changed, work completed, and rationale
4. Commit all changes with a descriptive git commit message following the format:
   ```
   feat(phase-X): complete task X.Y.Z — <brief description>
   ```

---

## What You CAN do independently

- Write all production code for tasks in ROADMAP.md
- Create files and directories per the structure in `PROJECT_STATE.md`
- Run tests and fix failing tests
- Fix bugs discovered during task execution
- Add inline code comments and docstrings
- Ask clarifying questions about a specific task implementation detail

---

## What You MUST escalate to AntiGravity

- Any task that seems architecturally inconsistent with the ADRs in `PROJECT_STATE.md`
- Any proposed change to the ROADMAP phase structure or acceptance criteria
- Any decision to use a library or technology not listed in the tech stack
- Blockers that cannot be resolved with standard debugging (e.g., API rate limits, missing credentials, unexpected platform behavior)
- Any decision to deviate from the directory structure in `PROJECT_STATE.md`

---

## Code Style Rules

### Python (backend + ml)
- Use type hints on all function signatures
- Format with `black` (line length 88)
- Sort imports with `isort`
- No `print()` in production code — use Python `logging` module
- All functions must have a one-line docstring

### JavaScript (extension)
- Use `const`/`let`, never `var`
- Use `async/await`, never raw `.then()` chains
- Add a JSDoc comment above every exported function
- No `console.log` in production code — remove before final commit

### TypeScript (dashboard)
- Strict mode enabled (`"strict": true` in tsconfig)
- No `any` types — use proper interfaces
- All API response types must be defined in `dashboard/lib/types.ts`

---

## Commit Message Format

```
feat(phase-N): complete task N.N.N — <description>
fix(phase-N): fix <what was broken>
test(phase-N): add tests for <component>
docs: update PROJECT_STATE.md / ROADMAP.md / CODEBASE_GUIDE.md
```

---

## Critical Reminders

> [!IMPORTANT]
> NEVER remove or bypass the SHAP explainability feature. It is the core differentiator of this project. If a task seems to conflict with SHAP, escalate to AntiGravity.

> [!WARNING]
> The `verdict` thresholds are: score > 0.7 = "phishing", 0.4–0.7 = "suspicious", < 0.4 = "legitimate". Do not change these without AntiGravity approval.

> [!NOTE]
> VirusTotal API (ADR-008) is used instead of raw WHOIS lookups. Always use a 5-second timeout with a graceful fallback (return `-1` for VT attributes) and cache results with `functools.lru_cache` to stay within rate limits. Never let an API timeout crash the `/analyze` endpoint.
